<description>
Overlay the two equal-size input masks and mark cells that are background in both.

Input structure
The grid is a vertical stack of two same-width rectangular masks.  Each half
contains the same background color plus exactly one foreground color.

Rule
Return one half's shape, filled with the background color, and recolor a
cell red when the corresponding cell is background in both input halves.

Key insight
The output is the common empty-space mask of the two stacked patterns, not
a crop or a transformation of either foreground object.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "two equal-size masks stacked vertically, sharing one background color",
  "transform": "collapse each pair of corresponding cells to one output cell: paint red where both source cells are background, otherwise background",
  "grid_shape": "height halved, same width"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 8 rows x 4 cols (two 4x4 halves stacked vertically) [observed constant]
  halves:
    structure: top half and bottom half share exactly one background color; each half has exactly one distinct foreground color
    bg_color: 0 (black) [observed]; solver requires exactly one shared color
    top_fg_color: 3 (green) [observed]; solver requires != red and != bg
    bot_fg_color: 1 (blue) [observed]; solver requires != red and != bg and != top_fg
    no_red: neither half contains color 2 (red) -- required by solver
    top_fg_cells: 4-11 out of 16 (25%-69% coverage)
    bot_fg_cells: 5-12 out of 16 (31%-75% coverage)
    connectivity: fg regions can have 1-4 connected components (no constraint)
    both_bg_cells: 1-4 cells where both halves are background (these become red in output)
  output:
    size: 4x4 (one half)
    fill: bg color everywhere, except cells where BOTH halves are background become red (2)
    red_cells: 1-4

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction (task is simple enough)
  steps:
    1. Set grid 8x4, bg=0, top_fg=3, bot_fg=1
    2. Generate top half (4x4) -- randomly place 4-11 green cells on black background
    3. Generate bottom half (4x4) -- randomly place 5-12 blue cells on black background
    4. Compute both_bg = cells where both halves are 0; reject if both_bg < 1 or both_bg > 4
    5. Stack top and bottom to form 8x4 input
  rejection_note: with typical coverage 25-75%, P(both_bg in 1..4) is high; low rejection expected
```
